@attribute [Route($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Disclosure}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Accessibility}")]

<PageTitle>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Disclosure @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</PageTitle>

<h1>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Disclosure @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Accessibility</h1>


<p>
    This Disclosure component was built following the W3C ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) for a simple disclosure pattern. By adhering to standardised patterns, we ensure that the component
    behaves predictably across different browsers and assistive technologies.
</p>
<p>
    The trigger is a native <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">button</span> element, so it can be focused and activated using a keyboard, touch, voice control or a mouse without any additional scripting.
    The <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">TriggerText</span> parameter value becomes its accessible name, and its <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-expanded</span> attribute is set to <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">true</span>
    or <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">false</span> to reflect whether the content is currently shown. A unique <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">id</span> attribute is generated for the content element and associated with
    the button via <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-controls</span>.
</p>
<p>
    The native <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">details</span> and <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">summary</span> elements were deliberately not used, as some screen readers announce the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">summary</span>
    element as a "disclosure triangle", even when its marker has been hidden with CSS. Unlike an accordion item, a disclosure doesn't need a heading, so the trigger isn't wrapped in one.
</p>
<p>
    When the content is collapsed it is hidden using CSS (<span class="@CodeSpan.Base">display: none</span>), which removes it from the accessibility tree and the tab order even when the
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">PersistContent</span> parameter keeps it in the DOM. By default the content element has no role, so it doesn't add to the landmarks a screen reader user can navigate.
    If the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ContentIsRegion</span> parameter is set to <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">true</span>, the content is given <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">role="region"</span> and is named
    by the trigger via <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-labelledby</span>, making it a landmark. This should be used sparingly, as each region adds another landmark to the page.
</p>
<p>
    When the <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">ContentMaxHeight</span> parameter is used and the content is taller than the limit, it scrolls within its own region. Some browsers don't make a scrollable region keyboard
    focusable on their own, which would leave keyboard users unable to scroll it. To avoid this, the Core auto-tabindex service watches the content element and, when it overflows and contains nothing focusable, gives it
    <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">tabindex="0"</span>, along with <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">role="group"</span> and an <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-label</span> matching the trigger text, so screen reader users
    know what has focus. A role or accessible name that is already present is never replaced, and the attributes are removed again when the content no longer overflows. Where the content contains links, buttons or other
    focusable elements, keyboard users can already reach and scroll it, so nothing is added.
</p>
<p>
    The optional icon is decorative. It is hidden from assistive technologies using <span class="@CodeSpan.Base">aria-hidden="true"</span>, so it doesn't affect the accessible name of the button.
</p>
<h2 id="disclosure-keyboard">Keyboard interaction</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="disclosure-keyboard" class="keyboard-list">
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Tab</kbd> - places focus on the trigger button. When the content is expanded, focus then moves through any focusable elements inside it, or onto the content
        itself if it scrolls and has nothing focusable inside.
    </li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Shift + Tab</kbd> reverses tab direction.</li>
    <li><kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Space or Enter</kbd> - when on the trigger, expands or collapses the content.</li>
    <li>
        <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Arrow keys</kbd> - <kbd class="@Kbd.Base @Kbd.Scheme(KbdScheme.Neutral)">Page Up, Page Down</kbd> - when a scrollable content region has focus, scroll it using the browser's standard behaviour.
    </li>
</ul>

<h2 id="device-testing">Accessibility device testing</h2>

<ul aria-labelledby="device-testing">
    <li>JAWS, Narrator and NVDA on Windows were each paired with every one of the following browsers: Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge and Mozilla Firefox.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on macOS was paired with Apple's Safari.</li>
    <li>VoiceOver on iPhone was paired with mobile Safari.</li>
    <li>Talkback on Android was paired with mobile Google Chrome.</li>
    <li>Voice access on Windows 11</li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> Testing was performed with the latest browser and assistive technologies versions that were available at the time of testing (see release history on the installation page for approximate testing dates)
</p>
<h2 id="screen-reader-quirks">Screen reader quirks or known issues</h2>
<ul aria-labelledby="screen-reader-quirks">
    <li>
        <b>Narrator</b> more often than not will not announce that the disclosure has been expanded after activation when
        paired with either Edge or Chrome, but is fine when paired with Firefox.
    </li>
    <li>
        <b>JAWS</b> paired with Firefox will sometimes repeat the trigger name and actions (expanded/collapsed).
        This is generally if the browser is opened and in use before JAWS has started.
    </li>
</ul>

<p>
    <strong>Note: </strong> The same quirks were also noted in the ARIA Authoring Practices Guide (APG) disclosure examples.
</p>

<div class="@FlexContent.Base @FlexContent.Wrap(UnitFlexWrap.Wrap) @LayoutAlignment.JustifyContent(UnitJustifyContent.SpaceBetween) @LayoutAlignment.AlignItems(UnitAlignItems.Centre)">
    <a class="@Button.Base @Button.LinkStyle @Gap.SetColGap(UnitGapSize.Four)" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Disclosure}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Installation}")">
        <span class="@SvgIcon.Base @SvgIcon.Size(UnitIconSize.ExtraLarge)" aria-hidden="true" style="--_svg-icon-source:var(--svg-left-arrow-circle-icon)"></span>
        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Disclosure - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Installation</span>

    </a>
    <a class="@Button.Base @Button.LinkStyle @Gap.SetColGap(UnitGapSize.Four)" href="@($"{GlobalValues.Root_path_Components}/{GlobalValues.Component_Path_Part_Disclosure}/{GlobalValues.Common_Page_Path_Api}")">

        <span>@GlobalValues.Component_Name_Disclosure - @GlobalValues.Common_Page_Title_Api</span>
        <span class="@SvgIcon.Base @SvgIcon.Size(UnitIconSize.ExtraLarge)" aria-hidden="true" style="--_svg-icon-source:var(--svg-right-arrow-circle-icon)"></span>
    </a>
</div>

